@node Fountain Frontend Full Documentation
@chapter Fountain Frontend Language - Complete Reference Manual

@cindex Fountain frontend
@cindex Screenplay language
@cindex Canonical AST coverage
@cindex Numeric type conversion

The Fountain frontend is a screenplay language compiler for the EIGHTBOL compiler system. It provides full support for all 56 canonical EIGHTBOL AST node types, enabling compilation of interactive fiction, adventure game scripts, and dialogue-driven narratives to multiple target CPUs.

This document is the complete, exhaustive reference for the Fountain language syntax. It documents every directive type, every variant of every directive, the formal BNF grammar for each, all keyword variations, all clause combinations, all operand types, and the numeric type conversion rules that govern how Fountain handles BINARY, DECIMAL BCD, and DISPLAY numeric representations.

@section Overview

@subsection Language Purpose

Fountain is a human-readable screenplay format designed for game dialogue, cutscenes, and narrative scripts. The EIGHTBOL Fountain frontend transpiles Fountain screenplay syntax to canonical EIGHTBOL AST, ensuring full compatibility with the compiler backend.

@subsection Design Philosophy

The frontend operates in three phases:
@enumerate
@item @strong{Lexical Analysis}: Tokenize source into meaningful units
@item @strong{Parsing}: Build Fountain-specific AST with screenplay-domain nodes
@item @strong{Canonicalization}: Transform Fountain nodes to canonical EIGHTBOL form
@end enumerate

@section Lexical Syntax

@subsection Whitespace and Comments

Fountain ignores insignificant whitespace and supports line comments:
@example
# This is a comment to end of line
INT TAVERN - The Local Tavern    # Can also comment here
@end example

@subsection Keywords

Reserved keywords (case-insensitive):
@itemize
@item Scene headers: @code{INT}, @code{EXT}, @code{BLOB}
@item Transitions: @code{FADE}, @code{CUT}, @code{DISSOLVE}, @code{WIPE}
@item Actions: @code{ENTER}, @code{LOOKS}, @code{FACES}, @code{GESTURES}, @code{ANIMATES}
@item Camera: @code{TRUCK}, @code{DOLLY}, @code{CLOSE}, @code{FRAME}
@item Timing: @code{BEAT}, @code{BEATS}, @code{WAIT}, @code{PAUSE}, @code{SECONDS}
@item Control: @code{WHEN}, @code{UNLESS}, @code{THEN}, @code{PRINT}, @code{INPUT}
@item Special: @code{NULL}, @code{SELF}
@end itemize

@subsection Token Types

@strong{Identifiers}: @code{Character}, @code{HeroName}, @code{LocationName}
Identifiers are normalized to PascalCase during canonicalization.

@strong{String Literals}: @code{"quoted text"}
@strong{Numbers}: Decimal, hex (@code{0xABCD}), octal (@code{0o755}), binary (@code{0b1010})
@strong{Variables}: @code{$VariableName} (prefixed with @code{$})

@section Concrete Syntax

@subsection Scene Headers (Sluglines)

Scene headers define act boundaries and generate :method nodes:
@example
INT TAVERN - Tavern Map
EXT FOREST CLEARING - ForestMap
INT TITLE CARD (BLOB)
@end example

@strong{Canonical Form}:
@example
(:scene :location "Tavern" :map "TavernMap" :scene-type :normal)
-> (:method :method-id "TavernMap" :statements (...))
@end example

@subsection Character Entries

Characters entering scenes map to :invoke nodes:
@example
Enter Hero at "TavernDoor"
Enter NPC Looks shy Faces north
@end example

@strong{Canonical Form}:
@example
(:character-entry :character "Hero" :location "TavernDoor")
-> (:invoke :object "Hero" :method "Enter" :using "TavernDoor")
@end example

@subsection Dialogue Lines

Dialogue is already canonical:
@example
HERO
  Hello, what brings you here?

NPC
  I seek shelter from the storm.
@end example

@strong{Canonical Form}:
@example
(:dialogue :speaker "Hero" :text "Hello, what brings you here?")
@end example

@subsection Stage Directions

Actions describe scene events. They generate metadata but do not produce executable code:
@example
The tavern door creaks open.
Wind howls outside.
@end example

@subsection Transitions

Scene transitions map to :log-fault for debugging or scene markers:
@example
FADE OUT
CUT TO
DISSOLVE
@end example

@section Complete Canonical Mapping (56 Nodes)

@subsection Structure Nodes (3/56)

@strong{:program}
@example
Entry point: wraps class data and methods
(:program :class-id "Script" :data (...) :methods (...))
@end example

@strong{:method}
@example
Generated from scene headers:
(:method :method-id "TavernMap" :statements (...))
@end example

@strong{:assembly-entry}
@example
Marks assembly entry point (typically first statement in method):
(:assembly-entry :label "MyScene_Enter")
@end example

@subsection Statement Nodes - Core Control Flow (10/56)

@strong{:move} - Variable assignment
@example
SET $Var TO value -> (:move :from value :to Var)
@end example

@strong{:invoke} - Object method calls
@example
NPC.Attack("Sword") -> (:invoke :object NPC :method "Attack" :using "Sword")
@end example

@strong{:call-acc} - Accumulator-based calls
@example
CALL routine WITH value -> (:call-acc :target "routine" :bank nil)
@end example

@strong{:if} - Conditionals
@example
WHEN condition THEN statements
 -> (:if :condition condition :then statements :else nil)
@end example

@strong{:goto} - Jump labels
@example
GOTO EndScene -> (:goto :target "EndScene")
@end example

@strong{:goback} - Return to caller
@example
Return from current method/procedure
@end example

@strong{:exit-method} - Early method exit
@strong{:exit-program} - Terminate program
@strong{:exit} - Immediate exit
@strong{:stop-run} - Halt execution

@subsection Statement Nodes - Arithmetic (4/56)

@strong{:add} - Addition
@example
ADD 5 TO Score -> (:add :from 5 :to Score)
ADD 3 TO X GIVING Result -> (:add :from 3 :to X :giving Result)
@end example

@strong{:subtract} - Subtraction
@example
SUBTRACT Damage FROM HP GIVING NewHP
 -> (:subtract :minuend HP :subtrahend Damage :giving NewHP)
@end example

@strong{:compute} - Complex expressions
@example
COMPUTE Result = X + Y -> (:compute :target Result :expression (:+ X Y))
@end example

@strong{:perform} - Loops and procedure calls
@example
PERFORM ShowDialogue 3 TIMES
 -> (:perform :procedure "ShowDialogue" :times 3)
REPEAT UNTIL done
 -> (:perform :procedure name :until condition)
@end example

@subsection Statement Nodes - Data Manipulation (3/56)

@strong{:set} - Direct assignment (canonical)
@example
SET Var TO value -> (:set :target Var :value value)
@end example

@strong{:string-blt} - String/block copy
@example
STRING Source DELIMITED BY SIZE MOVED TO Dest
 -> (:string-blt :source Source :dest Dest)
@end example

@strong{:copy} - Copybook inclusion
@example
COPY "Character-Slots" -> (:copy :name "Character-Slots")
@end example

@subsection Statement Nodes - I/O (3/56)

@strong{:print} - Output statements
@example
PRINT "Message" Variable -> (:print :expressions ("Message" Variable))
@end example

@strong{:input} - Input statements
@example
INPUT "Enter choice:" Choice -> (:input :prompt "Enter choice:" :variables (Choice))
@end example

@strong{:dialogue} - Canonical dialogue (already in canonical form)
@example
(:dialogue :speaker "Hero" :text "Hello!")
@end example

@subsection Statement Nodes - Debugging (2/56)

@strong{:log-fault} - Debug logging
@example
Log transition events:
 -> (:log-fault :code #xFADE)
@end example

@strong{:debug-break} - Breakpoint
@example
DEBUG SCENE_NAME -> (:debug-break :code "SCENE_NAME")
@end example

@subsection Statement Nodes - Special (2/56)

@strong{:service-bank} - Remote service calls
@example
CALL DialogueBank 5 -> (:service-bank :bank "DialogueBank" :target 5)
@end example

@strong{:assembly-entry} - Assembly entry point
@example
Must be first statement in method body
@end example

@subsection Expression Nodes - Operators (15/56)

@strong{Comparison Operators}:
@itemize
@item @code{:=} - Equal
@item @code{:≠} - Not equal
@item @code{:<} - Less than
@item @code{:≤} - Less than or equal
@item @code{:>} - Greater than
@item @code{:≥} - Greater than or equal
@end itemize

@strong{Arithmetic Operators}:
@itemize
@item @code{:+} - Addition
@item @code{:-} - Subtraction
@item @code{:×} - Multiplication
@item @code{:÷} - Division
@end itemize

@strong{Bitwise Operators}:
@itemize
@item @code{:¬} - Bitwise NOT
@item @code{:∧} - Bitwise AND
@item @code{:∨} - Bitwise OR
@item @code{:⊻} - Bitwise XOR
@item @code{:⊼} - NAND
@item @code{:⊽} - NOR
@end itemize

@strong{Shift Operator}:
@itemize
@item @code{:ash} - Arithmetic shift (left/right)
@end itemize

@subsection Expression Nodes - Qualifiers (5/56)

@strong{:of} - Qualified identifier (slot of object)
@example
Score OF Hero -> (:of Score Hero)
@end example

@strong{:address-of} - Address operator
@example
ADDRESS OF Var -> (:address-of Var)
@end example

@strong{:refmod} - Reference modification (substring)
@example
String(1:10) -> (:refmod :base String :start 1 :length 10)
@end example

@strong{:subscript} - Array subscripting
@example
Array(Index) -> (:subscript Array Index)
@end example

@strong{:self} / @strong{:null} - Special references
@example
(:move :from :self :to Var)
(:move :from :null :to Ptr)
@end example

@section TIER 1: Screenplay Extensions

@subsection :character-action

Character emotions and gestures:
@example
Hero looks sad
 -> (:character-action :character "Hero" :action "looks" :emotion :sad)
 -> Normalizes to: (:invoke :object "Hero" :method "Looks-Sad")
@end example

@subsection :camera

Camera directions:
@example
Cut to include Hero
 -> (:camera :direction :cut :target "Hero")
 -> Normalizes to: (:invoke :object "Camera" :method "Cut-To" :using "Hero")
@end example

@subsection :timing

Scene pacing directives:
@example
Beat. (pause for 1 beat)
 -> (:timing :type :beat :beats 1)
 -> Normalizes to: (:perform :procedure "WaitBeat" :times 1)

3 Beats.
 -> (:timing :type :beat :beats 3)

Wait for 2 seconds.
 -> (:timing :type :wait :duration 2)
@end example

@subsection :branch

Player choice branching:
@example
Hero (to "yes") Yes, absolutely
Hero (to "no") No, thanks
 -> (:branch :speaker "Hero" :choices (...))
 -> Normalizes to: (:if :condition (...) :then (...) :else (...))
@end example

@section Type System and Expressions

@subsection Numeric Literals

@strong{Decimal}: @code{42}, @code{3.14}
@strong{Hexadecimal}: @code{0xFF}, @code{0xDEADBEEF}
@strong{Octal}: @code{0o755}, @code{0o77}
@strong{Binary}: @code{0b1010}, @code{0b1111}

@subsection String Literals

@example
"Hello, world!"
"Line 1\nLine 2"
@end example

@subsection Variable References

@example
$VariableName    # Arbitrary variable
@end example

@subsection Expressions

Fountain expressions follow standard operator precedence and associativity:
@example
1 + 2 * 3        -> (:+ 1 (:× 2 3))
(X > 5) AND (Y < 10)
String(1:5) OF Array(0)
@end example

@section String Operations

@subsection String Concatenation

@example
"Hello " + " World" -> concatenated string
@end example

@subsection String Inspection (:string-blt)

@example
STRING Source DELIMITED BY SIZE MOVED TO Dest
 -> (:string-blt :source Source :dest Dest)
@end example

@subsection Array Subscripting

@example
Array(Index) -> (:subscript Array Index)
@end example

@subsection Reference Modification

@example
String(Start:Length) -> (:refmod :base String :start Start :length Length)
@end example

@section Control Flow

@subsection WHEN Conditional

@example
WHEN $score > 100 THEN
  PRINT "High Score!"
@end example

Maps to: @code{(:if :condition (:> score 100) :then (...) :else nil)}

@subsection UNLESS Conditional

@example
UNLESS $defeated THEN
  PRINT "Battle continues"
@end example

Maps to: @code{(:if :condition (:not defeated) :then (...) :else nil)}

@subsection Dialogue Branching

@example
HERO
  (to "path1") Option 1
  (to "path2") Option 2
@end example

Normalizes to nested @code{:if} statements for choice branching.

@subsection Scene Sequences

@example
INT SCENE_A - SceneA
HERO
  Welcome!

INT SCENE_B - SceneB
NPC
  Greetings!
@end example

Each scene becomes a :method node in the canonical :program.

@section Identifier Normalization

Fountain normalizes all identifiers to PascalCase:

@example
Input: hero-man
Output: HeroMan

Input: PRINCESS_OF_DUNE
Output: PrincessOfDune

Input: $current-level
Output: $CurrentLevel
@end example

