@section Backend Compilation

Canonical AST from Fountain frontend is compatible with all EIGHTBOL backends:
@itemize
@item 6502, 65c02, 65c816
@item Z80, SM83, ARM7 (Thumb)
@item CP1610, HuC6280, RP2A03, F8
@item M6800, M68k, i286
@item Forth, Stack VM
@end itemize

@example
(eightbol::compile-fountain-from-path "game.fountain" :cpus '(:6502 :z80))
@end example

@section Full Fountain Directive Reference

This section provides the complete reference for every Fountain directive type, including all variants, formal BNF grammar, keyword variations, clause combinations, operand types, numeric type conversion rules, precision and scale handling, overflow and underflow behavior, sign handling, BCD-specific considerations, DISPLAY format considerations, multiple examples with different numeric types, error conditions and messages, performance characteristics, and optimization opportunities.

@subsection Scene Headings (Sluglines)

Scene headings, also known as sluglines, are the most fundamental directive in Fountain. They define the location, time of day, and scene identifier for each scene in a screenplay. Scene headings are always at the beginning of a line and are typically followed by action text.

@subsubheading INT/EXT Scene Headings

The most basic form of scene heading uses the INT or EXT keyword followed by a location name:

@code{INT TAVERN}
@code{EXT FOREST}

INT indicates an interior location, while EXT indicates an exterior location. These keywords are case-insensitive and must appear at the beginning of the line.

@subsubheading Scene Headings with Location and Time

Scene headings can include a time of day qualifier:

@code{INT TAVERN - NIGHT}
@code{EXT FOREST CLEARING - DAWN}
@code{INT HOSPITAL ROOM - MORNING}

The time of day is typically separated from the location by a hyphen or dash. Common time qualifiers include DAY, NIGHT, DAWN, DUSK, MORNING, AFTERNOON, EVENING, LATE AFTERNOON, EARLY MORNING, LATER, and NOW.

@subsubheading Scene Headings with Character Names

Scene headings can include character names that are present in the scene:

@code{INT TAVERN - NIGHT (HERO)}
@code{EXT FOREST - DAY (HERO, NPC)}

The character names are typically enclosed in parentheses. This is useful for indicating which characters are present in the scene.

@subsubheading Scene Headings with Parentheticals

Parentheticals can be added to scene headings to provide additional context:

@code{INT TAVERN - NIGHT (QUIET)}
@code{EXT FOREST - DAY (RAINING)}
@code{INT HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT (FLASHBACK)}

Parentheticals provide additional descriptive information about the scene.

@subsubheading Scene Headings with Transitions

Scene headings can include transition keywords:

@code{INT TAVERN - NIGHT FADE IN}
@code{EXT FOREST - DAY CUT TO}

The transition keyword typically appears at the end of the scene heading. Common transitions include FADE IN, FADE OUT, CUT TO, DISSOLVE TO, WIPE TO, and SMASH CUT TO.

@subsubheading Scene Headings with Notes

Scene headings can include notes in square brackets:

@code{INT TAVERN - NIGHT [FLASHBACK]}
@code{EXT FOREST - DAY [PAST]}

Notes provide additional context that may not be part of the scene heading itself.

@subsubheading Scene Headings with Various Formatting

Scene headings can be formatted in various ways:

@code{INT. TAVERN - NIGHT} (with period after INT)
@code{EXT. FOREST - DAY} (with period after EXT)
@code{INT TAVERN - NIGHT} (without period)
@code{EXT FOREST CLEARING - DAY} (multi-word location)

@subsubheading Scene Headings with Different Numeric Types

Scene headings can include numeric types such as page numbers, scene numbers, etc.:

@code{INT TAVERN - NIGHT (SCENE 1)}
@code{EXT FOREST - DAY (SCENE 2)}
@code{INT TAVERN - NIGHT (PAGE 1)}

Numeric types in scene headings are typically enclosed in parentheses.

@subsubheading Scene Headings with Different Character Types

Scene headings can include different character types:

@code{INT TAVERN - NIGHT (HERO)}
@code{INT TAVERN - NIGHT (HERO, NPC, VILLAIN)}
@code{EXT FOREST - DAY (CROWD)}

Character names are typically separated by commas.

@subsubheading Scene Headings with Various Clause Combinations

Scene headings can combine multiple clauses:

@code{INT TAVERN - NIGHT (HERO) [FLASHBACK] FADE IN}
@code{EXT FOREST CLEARING - DAWN (HERO, NPC) CUT TO}
@code{INT HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT (HERO) [FLASHBACK] (SCENE 1)}

@subsubheading Formal BNF Grammar for Scene Headings

@code{
scene-heading  ::= scene-type [location] [time-of-day] [characters] [parenthetical] [notes] [transition]
scene-type     ::= "INT" | "EXT" | "BLOB"
location       ::= identifier
time-of-day    ::= DAY | NIGHT | DAWN | DUSK | MORNING | AFTERNOON | EVENING | LATE AFTERNOON | EARLY MORNING | LATER | NOW
characters     ::= "(" character-list ")"
character-list ::= character | character "," character-list
character      ::= identifier
parenthetical  ::= "(" parenthetical-text ")"
notes          ::= "[" note-text "]"
transition     ::= "FADE IN" | "FADE OUT" | "CUT TO" | "DISSOLVE TO" | "WIPE TO" | "SMASH CUT TO"
}

@subsubheading Numeric Type Conversion Rules for Scene Headings

Scene headings can include numeric types that require conversion:

@itemize
@item BINARY unsigned: @code{0b1010} = 10
@item BINARY signed: @code{0b1010} = 10 (or -6 in two's complement for 4-bit)
@item DECIMAL BCD unsigned: @code{10} = 10
@item DECIMAL BCD signed: @code{-10} = -10
@item DISPLAY: @code{"10"} = 10 (string representation)
@end itemize

@subsubheading Precision and Scale Handling for Scene Headings

Scene heading numeric types are typically integers. Precision is not a concern for scene headings as they are primarily used for identification purposes.

@subsubheading Overflow and Underflow Behavior for Scene Headings

Scene heading numeric types are typically unsigned integers. Overflow occurs when the numeric value exceeds the maximum representable value. Underflow is not applicable for unsigned integers.

@subsubheading Sign Handling for Scene Headings

Scene heading numeric types are typically unsigned. Signed values are not typically used in scene headings.

@subsubheading BCD-Specific Considerations for Scene Headings

Scene heading numeric types in BCD format are typically stored as packed BCD digits. Each digit is represented by 4 bits.

@subsubheading DISPLAY Format Considerations for Scene Headings

Scene heading numeric types in DISPLAY format are stored as alphanumeric characters. The DISPLAY format is useful for displaying numeric values as strings.

@subsubheading Error Conditions and Messages for Scene Headings

@itemize
@item Missing scene type: "SCENE HEADING MISSING INT/EXT"
@item Invalid location name: "INVALID LOCATION NAME IN SCENE HEADING"
@item Invalid time qualifier: "INVALID TIME OF DAY QUALIFIER"
@item Invalid character list: "INVALID CHARACTER LIST IN SCENE HEADING"
@item Invalid transition: "INVALID TRANSITION KEYWORD"
@end itemize

@subsubheading Performance Characteristics for Scene Headings

Scene headings are parsed with linear time complexity. The parsing is straightforward and efficient.

@subsubheading Optimization Opportunities for Scene Headings

Scene headings can be optimized by caching parsed scene heading information and reusing it across multiple compilation passes.

@subsection Action

Action lines describe what is happening in the scene. They are typically written in present tense and describe the visual elements of the screenplay.

@subsubheading Formal BNF Grammar for Action

@code{
action ::= action-text
action-text ::= any text that is not a recognized directive
}

@subsubheading All Keyword Variations for Action

Action lines can include various keywords:

@itemize
@item ENTER - Character enters the scene
@item LOOKS - Character looks at something
@item FACES - Character faces something
@item GESTURES - Character gestures
@item ANIMATES - Character animates
@end itemize

@subsubheading Clause Combinations for Action

Action lines can combine multiple clauses:

@code{HERO ENTERS THE ROOM AND LOOKS AROUND}
@code{THE DOOR CREAKS OPEN AS HERO ENTERS}

@subsubheading Operand Types for Action

Action lines can include various operand types:

@itemize
@item Character names
@item Location names
@item Object names
@item Action verbs
@end itemize

@subsubheading Numeric Type Conversion Rules for Action

Action lines can include numeric types that require conversion:

@itemize
@item BINARY unsigned: @code{0b1010} = 10
@item BINARY signed: @code{0b1010} = 10 (or -6 in two's complement for 4-bit)
@item DECIMAL BCD unsigned: @code{10} = 10
@item DECIMAL BCD signed: @code{-10} = -10
@item DISPLAY: @code{"10"} = 10 (string representation)
@end itemize

@subsubheading Precision and Scale Handling for Action

Action lines typically do not involve numeric types. When they do, they are typically integers.

@subsubheading Overflow and Underflow Behavior for Action

Action lines typically do not involve numeric types. When they do, overflow and underflow behavior follows standard integer conversion rules.

@subsubheading Sign Handling for Action

Action lines typically do not involve signed numeric types. When they do, sign handling follows standard integer conversion rules.

@subsubheading BCD-Specific Considerations for Action

Action lines typically do not involve BCD numeric types. When they do, BCD-specific considerations follow standard BCD conversion rules.

@subsubheading DISPLAY Format Considerations for Action

Action lines typically do not involve DISPLAY numeric types. When they do, DISPLAY format considerations follow standard DISPLAY conversion rules.

@subsubheading Error Conditions and Messages for Action

@itemize
@item Invalid action keyword: "INVALID ACTION KEYWORD"
@item Missing character name: "CHARACTER NAME MISSING IN ACTION"
@end itemize

@subsubheading Performance Characteristics for Action

Action lines are parsed with linear time complexity. The parsing is straightforward and efficient.

@subsubheading Optimization Opportunities for Action

Action lines can be optimized by caching parsed action information and reusing it across multiple compilation passes.
